# Contributing to Pulse Studio CRM

Thank you for your interest in contributing to Pulse! This document provides guidelines and instructions for contributing.

## Code of Conduct

Please be respectful and constructive in all interactions.

## Getting Started

1. Fork the repository
2. Clone your fork locally:
   ```bash
   git clone https://github.com/yourusername/pulse-studio-crm.git
   cd pulse-studio-crm
   ```
3. Create a feature branch:
   ```bash
   git checkout -b feature/your-feature-name
   ```

## Development Workflow

### Setting Up Your Environment

```bash
# No build step required! Just open index.html in a browser
# Or use a local server:
python -m http.server 8000
```

### File Structure Guidelines

- **HTML**: Semantic, accessible markup in `index.html`
- **CSS**: Component-scoped styles in `css/styles.css`
- **JavaScript**: Modular organization in `js/` directory:
  - `data.js` — Data structures and app state
  - `auth.js` — Authentication logic
  - `modal.js` — Modal dialogs
  - `forms.js` — Form handling
  - `render.js` — UI rendering
  - `app.js` — Application logic

## Code Style

### JavaScript
- Use camelCase for variables and functions
- Keep functions focused and single-purpose
- Add JSDoc comments for public functions
- Use `const` by default, `let` when needed

Example:
```javascript
/**
 * Opens a member creation modal
 */
function openAddMemberModal() {
  openModal(`...`);
}
```

### CSS
- Use CSS custom properties (variables) for colors
- Follow BEM naming for modular components
- Mobile-first approach with media queries
- Keep specificity low

Example:
```css
.button {
  background: var(--coral);
}

.button:hover {
  background: var(--coral-dk);
}

@media(max-width: 570px) {
  .button {
    padding: 10px;
  }
}
```

### HTML
- Use semantic elements (`<header>`, `<section>`, `<nav>`, etc.)
- Include ARIA labels for accessibility
- Keep inline event handlers minimal (consider refactoring to event listeners)

## Making Changes

### Feature Development

1. **Create a branch** with a descriptive name:
   ```bash
   git checkout -b feature/member-export-to-pdf
   ```

2. **Keep commits atomic** — each commit should represent one logical change

3. **Update documentation** if your changes affect how the app works

4. **Test thoroughly** in different browsers and screen sizes

### Before Submitting

- [ ] Code follows the style guidelines above
- [ ] No console errors or warnings
- [ ] Tested on desktop, tablet, and mobile
- [ ] Changes don't break existing functionality
- [ ] README updated if needed
- [ ] Commits have clear messages

## Pull Request Process

1. Push your branch to your fork
2. Create a Pull Request with a clear title and description
3. Include a reference to any related issues
4. Wait for review
5. Address feedback and make requested changes
6. Once approved, your PR will be merged

### PR Title Format

```
[Feature|Fix|Docs|Refactor] Brief description of changes
```

Examples:
- `[Feature] Add member bulk import from CSV`
- `[Fix] Correct responsive layout on mobile`
- `[Docs] Update API documentation`

### PR Description Template

```markdown
## Description
Brief description of what this PR does.

## Related Issues
Closes #123

## Changes
- Specific change 1
- Specific change 2
- Specific change 3

## Testing
How you tested these changes.

## Screenshots (if applicable)
Before/after screenshots.
```

## Testing Guidelines

### Manual Testing
- Test in Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and Edge
- Test responsive behavior:
  - Desktop (1920px+)
  - Tablet (768px-900px)
  - Mobile (320px-567px)
- Test all new features thoroughly
- Test edge cases (empty data, long names, etc.)

### Demo Accounts
Use these for testing:
- Owner: `owner` / `owner123`
- Staff: `staff` / `staff123`

## Documentation

### Code Comments
- Add comments for complex logic
- Explain "why" not just "what"
- Keep comments up-to-date

Example:
```javascript
// Truncate names to prevent layout overflow in mobile view
const truncated = name.length > 20 ? name.substring(0, 17) + '...' : name;
```

### README Updates
If your feature adds functionality, update the README:
- Add to features list
- Include usage examples if needed
- Update roadmap if appropriate

## Reporting Issues

### Bug Reports
Include:
- Clear description of the bug
- Steps to reproduce
- Expected behavior
- Actual behavior
- Browser and OS
- Screenshots if applicable

### Feature Requests
Include:
- Clear description of desired feature
- Use case and motivation
- Suggested implementation approach
- Wireframes/mockups if helpful

## Questions?

Feel free to:
- Open a discussion in GitHub Discussions
- Ask in an issue
- Comment on a related PR

## Recognition

Contributors will be recognized in:
- The README contributors section
- Release notes for their contributions

